response:
 [asy]
unitsize (3 cm);

pair A, B, C, D;

A = (0,0);
B = (1,0);
C = (1,-1);
D = (0,-1);

draw(A--B--C--D--cycle);
draw(A--D);

label("$A$", A, SW);
label("$B$", B, SE);
label("$C$", C, NE);
label("$D$", D, NW);
label("$62^\circ$", (A + B)/2, S);
label("$75^\circ$", (A + D)/2, W);
[/asy]

Because $\overline{AB} \parallel \overline{CD}$ and $\overline{BC} \parallel \overline{AD},$ opposite angles of parallelogram $ABCD$ are equal, so $\angle B = \angle D = 180^\circ - \angle A = 118^\circ.$

Since the angles in $\triangle ABD$ add to $180^\circ,$ we have
\[180^\circ = \angle A + \angle ABD + \angle BDA = 62^\circ + \angle ABD + 75^\circ.\]Then $\angle ABD = 180^\circ - 62^\circ - 75^\circ = 43^\circ.$

Finally, because $\overline{BC} \parallel \overline{AD},$ we have
\[\angle BDC = \angle ABD = \boxed{43^\circ}.\]

 [asy]

pair A,B,C,D;

A = (0,0);

B = (1,0);

D = rotate(105)*(0.6,0);

C = D+B-A;

draw(A--B--C--D--A);

label("$A$",A,SW);

label("$B$",B,SE);

label("$D$",D,N);

label("$C$",C,NE);

draw(A--D,dashed);

label("$75^\circ$",D/2,W);

[/asy]

Because $\overline{AB}\parallel\overline{CD}$, we have $\angle B + \angle C = 180^\circ$.  Also, because $\overline{AD}\parallel\overline{BC}$, we have $\angle A + \angle D = 180^\circ$.  Hence $\angle C = 180^\circ - \angle B$ and $\angle D = 180^\circ - \angle A$.  Then the equation $\angle A + \angle D = 180^\circ$ gives us $180^\circ - \angle B + \angle A = 180^\circ$, or $\angle A = \angle B$.  Then $\angle B = 62^\circ$, so $\angle BDC = 180^\circ - \angle B - \angle C = 180^\circ - 62^\circ - 75^\circ = \boxed{43^\circ}$.
The answer is $\boxed{43}$.

