label("75", D + (0.5, -0.5));
draw(D--B, linewidth(1));
label("$x$", D + (0.2, 0.2));
[/asy]

Since $ABCD$ is a parallelogram, opposite angles are equal, so $\angle C = \angle A = 62^\circ$.

Since $AB \parallel CD$, alternate interior angles are equal, so $\angle DAB = \angle BDC = 75^\circ$.

The angles of $\triangle BDC$ add up to $180^\circ$, so $62^\circ + 75^\circ + x = 180^\circ$ and $x = 180^\circ - 62^\circ - 75^\circ = \boxed{43^\circ}$.

 [asy]
pair A,B,C,D;

A = (0,0);
B= (1,0);
D = rotate(105)*(0.6,0);
C = D+B-A;
draw(A--B--C--D--A);
label("$A$",A,S);
label("$B$",B,S);
label("$C$",C,NE);
label("$D$",D,NW);
draw(A--D);
label("$62^\circ$",A+(0,-0.2),S);
label("$75^\circ$",D+(0.1,-0.15),W);
[/asy]

We know that opposite angles in a parallelogram are equal, so $\angle C = \angle A = 62^\circ.$

Since $\overline{AB}\parallel\overline{CD},$ we have $\angle A + \angle ADB + \angle BDC = 180^\circ$ (The angles on a line add to $180^\circ.$)  So, substituting, we have $62^\circ + 75^\circ + \angle BDC=180^\circ.$

So, $137^\circ + \angle BDC=180^\circ$ and $\angle BDC = 180^\circ - 137^\circ = \boxed{43^\circ}.$.
The answer is $\boxed{43}$.

