\begin{align*}
p(x) &= (x^2 + 2ax + b)^2 + 2a(x^2 + 2ax + b) - b \\
&= (x^2 + 2ax + b)(x^2 + 2ax + b + 2a) - b \\
&= (x^2 + 2ax + b)(x^2 + 2(a + 1)x + (b + 2a)) - b.
\end{align*}Thus, the roots of $p(x)$ consist of the roots of $x^2 + 2ax + b = 0$ and $x^2 + 2(a + 1)x + (b + 2a) = 0.$

Furthermore, the polynomial $p(x)$ has exactly one real root if and only if one of the following two conditions holds:
\begin{itemize}
  \item Exactly one of the quadratic equations has one real root, and the other quadratic equation has no real roots.
  \item Both quadratic equations have exactly one real root, but the real roots are not the same.
\end{itemize}

Now, the quadratic equation $x^2 + 2ax + b = 0$ has one real root if and only if its discriminant is nonnegative.  This discriminant is $4a^2 - 4b = 4(a^2 - b),$ so $a^2 - b \ge 0.$

The quadratic equation $x^2 + 2(a + 1)x + (b + 2a) = 0$ has one real root if and only if its discriminant is nonnegative.  This discriminant is $4(a + 1)^2 - 4(b + 2a) = 4[(a + 1)^2 - (b + 2a)],$ so $(a + 1)^2 - (b + 2a) \ge 0.$

Thus, the solutions $(a, b)$ must satisfy
\[a^2 - b \ge 0 \quad \text{and} \quad (a + 1)^2 - (b + 2a) \ge 0.\]These inequalities give us two parabolas in the $ab$-plane.

