pair A, B, C;

A = (0,0);
B = (50,0);
C = (0,12);

draw(A--B--C--cycle);

label("$A$", A, SW);
label("$B$", B, SE);
label("$C$", C, N);
label("$50$", (A + B)/2, S);
label("$50$", (A + C)/2, W);
label("$2\arcsin\left(\frac{24}{25}\right)$", (0.6,0.2));
[/asy]

The area of a triangle is given by the formula $A = \frac{1}{2} \cdot \text{base} \cdot \text{height}$. In this case, the base is $AC$ with length 50 cm. To find the height, we can drop a perpendicular from $B$ to $AC$ and label the intersection point as $D$.

[asy]
unitsize(1 cm);

pair A, B, C, D;

A = (0,0);
B = (50,0);
C = (0,12);
D = (0,0);

draw(A--B--C--cycle);
draw(B--D);

label("$A$", A, SW);
label("$B$", B, SE);
label("$C$", C, N);
label("$D$", D, S);
label("$50$", (A + B)/2, S);
label("$50$", (A + C)/2, W);
label("$2\arcsin\left(\frac{24}{25}\right)$", (0.6,0.2));
[/asy]

Now we have right triangle $ABD$ with $AD$ as the height and $BD$ as the base. We know that $BD$ is the height of the triangle $ABC$. Since $\angle BAD = 2\arcsin\left(\frac{24}{25}\right)$, we can use the sine function to find the ratio of the opposite side (height $AD$) to the hypotenuse (side $AB$).

We have $\sin(\angle BAD) = \frac{AD}{AB}$. Solving for $AD$ gives $AD = AB \cdot \sin(\angle BAD)$.

Substituting the given values, we get $AD = 50 \cdot \sin\left(2\arcsin\left(\frac{24}{25}\right)\right)$.

The sine of an angle twice an angle is $2\sin(\theta)\cos(\theta)$, so we have $\sin\left(2\arcsin\left(\frac{24}{25}\right)\right) = 2\sin\left(\arcsin\left(\frac{24}{25}\right)\right)\cos\left(\arcsin\left(\frac{24}{25}\right)\right)$.

We know $\sin\left(\arcsin\left(\frac{24}{25}\right)\right) = \frac{24}{25}$ and $\cos\left(\arcsin\left(\frac{24}{25}\right)\right) = \sqrt{1 - \sin^2\left(\arcsin\left(\frac{24}{25}\right)\right)} = \sqrt{1 - \left(\frac{24}{25}\right)^2} = \frac{7}{25}$, so we have $2\sin\left(\arcsin\left(\frac{24}{25}\right)\right)\cos\left(\arcsin\left(\frac{24}{25}\right)\right) = 2 \cdot \frac{24}{25} \cdot \frac{7}{25} = \frac{336}{625}$.

Substituting this back into the equation for $AD$, we get $AD = 50 \cdot \frac{336}{625} = \frac{336}{12.5} = 26.88$ cm.

Finally, we can calculate the area of triangle $ABC$ as $A = \frac{1}{2} \cdot \text{base} \cdot \text{height} = \frac{1}{2} \cdot 50 \cdot 26.88 = 672$ square cm.

So the area of triangle $ABC$ is $\boxed{672}$ square cm.

