By the Pythagorean Theorem, $AC = \sqrt{AB^2 - BC^2} = \sqrt{400 - 256} = 4\sqrt{14}.$

Hence,
\[\tan A = \frac{BD}{AD} = \frac{16}{\sqrt{400 - 256}} = \frac{16}{\sqrt{144}} = \frac{16}{12} = \frac43.\]
This value is achievable, as can be seen by taking $A$ to be an acute angle in a $3-4-5$ right triangle.  Hence, the largest possible value of $\tan A$ is $\boxed{\frac{4}{3}}.$

 The maximum value of $\tan A$ occurs when $A$ is the largest angle.  In this case, $A$ is an angle of a right triangle with hypotenuse $AC$ and leg $BC.$

