Then by the Law of Sines on triangle $ACD,$
\[\frac{AC}{\sin \angle CAD} = \frac{AD}{\sin \angle CDA}.\]Then
\[\sin \angle CDA = \frac{AD \sin \angle CAD}{AC}.\]By the Law of Sines on triangle $ABC,$
\[\sin \angle CDA = \frac{BC \sin \angle BAC}{AC} = \frac{16 \sin \angle BAC}{20} = \frac{4}{5} \sin \angle BAC.\]Also, $\angle CDA = \angle ADB,$ so
\[\sin \angle ADB = \frac{4}{5} \sin \angle BAC.\]Then
\begin{align*}
\tan A &= \tan (\angle ADB + \angle BAC) \\
&= \frac{\tan \angle ADB + \tan \angle BAC}{1 - \tan \angle ADB \tan \angle BAC} \\
&= \frac{\frac{\sin \angle ADB}{\cos \angle ADB} + \frac{\sin \angle BAC}{\cos \angle BAC}}{1 - \frac{\sin \angle ADB \sin \angle BAC}{\cos \angle ADB \cos \angle BAC}} \\
&= \frac{\frac{4}{5} \sin \angle BAC + \frac{\sin \angle BAC}{\cos \angle BAC}}{1 - \frac{4}{5} \sin \angle BAC} \\
&= \frac{\frac{4}{5} \sin \angle BAC + \cos \angle BAC}{1 - \frac{4}{5} \sin \angle BAC}.
\end{align*}In triangle $ABC,$
\[\cos^2 \angle BAC + \sin^2 \angle BAC = 1,\]so
\[\cos \angle BAC = \sqrt{1 - \sin^2 \angle BAC} = \sqrt{1 - \left( \frac{16 - x}{20} \right)^2} = \sqrt{1 - \frac{256 - 32x + x^2}{400}} = \sqrt{\frac{144 + 32x - x^2}{400}}.\]Then
\begin{align*}
\tan A &= \frac{\frac{4}{5} \cdot \frac{16 - x}{20} + \sqrt{\frac{144 + 32x - x^2}{400}}}{1 - \frac{4}{5} \cdot \frac{16 - x}{20}} \\
&= \frac{\frac{32 - 4x + 12 \sqrt{144 + 32x - x^2}}{200}}{\frac{200 - 320 + 4x}{200}} \\
&= \frac{32 - 4x + 12 \sqrt{144 + 32x - x^2}}{4x - 80 + 12 \sqrt{144 + 32x - x^2}} \\
&= \frac{4 (8 - x) + 12 \sqrt{144 + 32x - x^2}}{4 (x - 20) + 12 \sqrt{144 + 32x - x^2}}.
\end{align*}Thus, $\tan A$ is maximized when $x = 8.$  This makes $\angle BAC$ a right angle, so
\[\tan A = \frac{16}{20 - x} = \frac{16}{12} = \boxed{\frac{4}{3}}.\]

 Note that when the vector $\begin{pmatrix} 1 \\ 2 \\ 2 \end{pmatrix}$ is rotated $\arccos (1/3)$ about the origin, it sweeps out a cone.  Furthermore, since it passes through the $x$-axis, at that moment it is traveling in the $x$-direction.  In other words, the vector is instantaneously pointing along the $x$-axis at that moment.

[asy]
import three;

size(180);
currentprojection = perspective(6,3,2);

triple I = (1,0,0), J = (0,1,0), K = (0,0,1), O = (0,0,0);
triple P = (1/sqrt(6),(1/sqrt(2)),(1/sqrt(3)));
triple Q = (1,0,0);

draw(O--3*I, Arrow3(6));
draw(O--3*J, Arrow3(6));
draw(O--3*K, Arrow3(6));
draw(O--P,Arrow3(6));
draw(O--Q,Arrow3(6));
draw(P--Q,dashed);

label("$x$", 2.2*I);
label("$y$", 2.2*J);
label("$z$", 2.2*K);

dot("$(1,1,1)$", P, S);
dot("$(1,0,0)$", Q, S);
[/asy]

The vector $\begin{pmatrix} 1 \\ 2 \\ 2 \end{pmatrix}$ has magnitude $\sqrt{1^2 + 2^2 + 2^2} = 3$, so the instantaneous velocity vector is proportional to $\begin{pmatrix} 1 \\ 0 \\ 0 \end{pmatrix}$.

In other words, if the vector instantaneously points along the $x$-axis, then it must be of the form $\begin{pmatrix} 3 \\ 0 \\ 0 \end{pmatrix}$ at that moment.  Therefore, the vector $\begin{pmatrix} 1 \\ 2 \\ 2 \end{pmatrix}$ rotated $\arccos (1/3)$ about the origin and passing through the $x$-axis is $\boxed{\begin{pmatrix} 3 \\ 0 \\ 0 \end{pmatrix}}$.
